The '''Reformist Bloc''' ({{lang-bl|Bloc Reformista}}) was a political coalition of [[W:social democracy|social democrats]] within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] that embraced [[W:Idealism in international relations|international idealism]] and sought to gradually open Balisca to the global market and eventually establish a [[W:Socialist market economy|socialist market economy]]. 

They were generally placed at the [[W:Center-left politics|center-left]] position, and espoused the principles of [[W:Alternative globalization|alternative globalization]]. After the [[W:Great Recession|Great Recession]] and the [[W:Conservative wave|blue tide]] in Latin America the Reformist Bloc gradually shifted further to the left, largely as a response to the global resurgence of [[W:Neo-nationalism|nationalism]] and [[W:Right-wing populism|right-wing populism]].

In the summer of 2021 the majority of the Baliscan electorate (including the overwhelming majority of self-identified [[Federalism in Balisca|federalists]]) supported the next confederal elections being moved forward; which ultimately [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|occurred in October]]. In the elections the [[Reformist Bloc]] lost all of it's seats, and a significant number of the delegates previously aligned with the Bloc were defeated as the electorate rallied around anti-capitalist, anti-statist and [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] candidates.

The organization subsequently dissolved in January 2022, after suspending it's activities in September 2021 following the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|resignation]] of [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]] [[Andrea Palacios]].

